{"event": "editor_note", "time": 1791882905.123, "text": "All violations are resolved. Seeds 11 and 12 both pass with 0 violations.\n\n## Summary\n\n### Root Cause\n\n**Bug in `src/jobrouter/store.py`, `load()` method (line 38).**\n\n`store.load()` skipped records with `units=None` (precancel tombstones) during router restart recovery. After a restart, these tombstones were absent from the in-memory `self.jobs` dict. When a `submit` for the same key arrived later, `store.lookup()` returned `None`, causing the router to create a fresh QUEUED job \u2014 which was then dispatched, leased, and had its GPU operation submitted and run to SUCCEEDED. This violated invariant A2: a CANCELLED job (acknowledged precancel) had a GPU operation that SUCCEEDED.\n\n### Evidence\n\n- `router-1.log`: `job_terminal key=[REDACTED] state=CANCELLED reason=precancel` at t=3.007 (cancel before submit \u2192 tombstone with `units=None`)\n- Violations: `job D/n0: op D/n0/a1 accepted at 40.0838 after cancel acknowledged at 3.0077` \u2014 the GPU op was submitted ~37 seconds after the cancel, consistent with a router restart losing the tombstone from memory and a delayed submit creating a new QUEUED job\n- Seeds 12\u201315 showed the same pattern with more crashes (up to 8 affected jobs per run)\n\n### Fix\n\nRemoved the `job[\"units\"] is None` guard in `store.load()` so tombstones are loaded into `self.jobs`. They are CANCELLED so they are never enqueued. When a submit arrives for a precancelled key, `store.lookup()` now finds the tombstone and the existing handler correctly fills in `units`/`duration` while keeping the job CANCELLED. The job count return value was adjusted to exclude tombstones (units=None) to preserve the recovery summary semantics.\n\n### Investigators' Hypotheses\n\n- **state-recovery** (acquire_cache not restored after restart): Not a bug \u2014 the router-restart runbook explicitly documents this as expected: \"Acquire replies cached before the crash are gone; a worker whose acquire reply was lost simply acquires again, and the orphaned grant expires.\"\n- **leases-membership** (`HolderLease.extend` guard): The `if until > self.valid_until` guard is conservative but not the cause of the A2 violations. The A2 violations are entirely explained by the tombstone-loss bug.\n- **delivery-commit** (report retry / gpu_client lease checks): Not the cause of the A2 violations; the violations are about precancelled jobs running, not about holder actions after lease expiry."}
