{"event": "editor_note", "time": 1791884030.509, "text": "Now I have a clear picture. The bug is confirmed:\n\n**Bug:** `membership.py` lines 88-89 compare a worker-local clock timestamp (`sent`) against the coordinator's local clock (`self.node.clock.now()`). The clock offset between nodes can be up to 0.5s (per operational notes \u00a71), but `HEARTBEAT_RPC_TIMEOUT_S = 0.4s`. When a worker's clock is behind coord's by more than 0.4s, every heartbeat from that worker appears \"stale\" to coord and is silently dropped (returns `info, False` without updating `last_hb`). This causes the silence timer to expire, spuriously fencing the worker, which then re-registers \u2014 causing the excessive `register` RPC count violation.\n\nThe operational notes \u00a79 explicitly state: \"coord judges a heartbeat only by when it arrives, on its own clock. It never compares a timestamp taken on a worker with its own clock.\"\n\n**Fix:** Remove the cross-node clock comparison (lines 88-89 in `membership.py`) and remove the `\"sent\"` field from the heartbeat payload in `worker.py` line 79."}
